Subject: [M155] [FSA] Fix use-after-free in FileSystemAccessLockManager

Original change's description:
> [FSA] Fix use-after-free in FileSystemAccessLockManager
>
> Problem:
> Consider three locks on the same path:
> - Lock A: Active lock held by a BFCached page.
> - Lock B: Pending lock requested by another BFCached page. Lock A begins
>   eviction, and Lock B's callback holds the only surviving handle to
>   Lock A (`Lock B->evicting_subroot_lock_ = Lock A`).
> - Lock C: New pending lock requested by an active page, superseding
>   Lock B in its parent's `child_locks_`.
>
> In `Lock C->SetEvictingSubrootLock(Lock B)`, Lock A was extracted into a
> stack local `next_evicting_subroot_lock` while calling
> `evicting_subroot_lock->EvictPendingSubtree()` on Lock B. Evicting
> Lock B dropped its callbacks, releasing the last handle to Lock A and
> invoking `Lock A->LockHandleDestroyed()`.
>
> Because Lock C had not yet adopted Lock A as its
> `evicting_subroot_lock_`, `Lock A->IsEvictingSubroot()` returned false.
> Believing it was an active lock, Lock A invoked `DestroySelf()`, which
> called `ReleaseChild()` on its parent (`root_lock_`). This erased Lock C
> from `root_lock_->child_locks_` while Lock C's
> `SetEvictingSubrootLock()` was still running on the stack. When
> `EvictPendingSubtree()` returned, Lock C accessed its now-freed memory
> to assign `evicting_subroot_lock_`, causing a heap use-after-free.
>
> Additionally, two related lifecycle bugs existed:
> 1. `LockHandleDestroyed()` unconditionally called `DestroySelf()` for
>    locks with empty handles, even if they were detached/superseded
>    (`parent->GetChild(path) != this`) or still anchored child locks in
>    the path hierarchy (`!child_locks_.empty()`).
> 2. An unhandled null pointer occurred in `IsEvictingSubroot()` when
>    `parent_lock_->GetChild(path_)` was null.
>
> Fix:
> 1. In `SetEvictingSubrootLock()`: Adopt `next_evicting_subroot_lock`
>    (Lock A) into `evicting_subroot_lock_` before calling
>    `EvictPendingSubtree()` on Lock B. During eviction, Lock A's
>    `IsEvictingSubroot()` evaluates to true, so Lock A destroys itself
>    safely via `pending_lock->RemoveEvictingSubrootLock()` and promotes
>    Lock C, without invoking `ReleaseChild()`.
> 2. In `LockHandleDestroyed()`:
>    - Do not destroy a lock if
>      `!child_locks_.empty() || evicting_subroot_lock_`. Intermediate
>      path nodes must be preserved until their children are gone to
>      avoid dangling parent references.
>    - Verify parent ownership (`parent_lock_->GetChild(path_) == this`)
>      before calling `DestroySelf()`. Detached or superseded locks cannot
>      delete unrelated children from former parents.
> 3. In `ReleaseChild()`: Prune ancestor locks whose children are now
>    empty if they have no remaining handles or active evictions.
> 4. In `IsEvictingSubroot()`: Add a null check for
>    `parent_lock_->GetChild(path_)` to handle cases where `path_` has
>    already been released or detached.
> 5. In `IteratePendingSubtreeCallbacks()`: Guard callback execution with
>    `!pending_callbacks_.empty()` to support locks promoted reentrantly
>    before callbacks are attached.
>
> AGY was used to help write this patch.
>
